  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 123 AM EDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nSynoptic situation looks fairly well understood, with a trough \ncurrently cutting off from upper westerlies, proceeding to hang \naround a couple of days, and then dissipating and being re-absorbed \ninto the westerlies by late week. Next weekend potentially another \nMidwestern trough will be present, though the two ridges (one in the \nWest, one in the Atlantic) may try to bridge that gap. \n\nThe end result of our slow moving upper friend is we will keep rain \nchances in the forecast every day over the next several days, and \nlikely into the next weekend. Most of the activity will be diurnally \nfocused, that is centered in the afternoon and early evening hours, \nbut as we've seen this past evening, shower activity can continue \ninto the nighttime hours, just with lesser coverage. Convection \nmight be, on some days, focused moreso on the large terrain features \nwe have and less so in the valley, similar to how Monday progressed, \nbut each day will be dependent on the degree of lift we can obtain \nfrom the resident upper low, any outflow boundaries, and available \ninstability.\n\nAs far as risks, steering motions are on the low side this week, so \ncannot rule out an isolated flood risk. PWATs aren't overly high, \nbut the slow storm motions are enough coupled with the otherwise \nsatisfactory moisture content to present a very low risk. Should any \nday realize more CAPE available for storm growth, then a few gusty \nwinds in collapsing downdrafts is also possible, but with our low \nshear environment I think that is on the low side as well. And on \ndays when storms get tall enough, lightning of course. Otherwise \napart from storms we look to have standard August temperatures and \ngenerally light summer winds.",
